        ":question/problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {{u}^{2}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
